1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Cold hard cash.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Whether it jingles in our pockets or folds in our wallets,

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
it flows by the billions from government agencies

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
that have mastered the craft of making it.

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Empowering consumers and confounding counterfeiters.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
The result of time-honored technology,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
painstaking artistry, and precision automation.

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, Money on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
They say that it can't buy happiness.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
And that the love of it is the root of all evil.

11
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:58,000
But most of us seem to be obsessed with earning, saving and spending it.

12
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:03,000
For better or worse, money is the measure of all things in America.

13
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:09,000
And two agencies work around the clock, satisfying our demand for it.

14
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:13,000
The Bureau of Engraving and Printing produces our paper currency.

15
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:19,000
And the United States Mint, the largest mint in the world, manufactures our coins.

16
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:23,080
We are the largest mint in large measure because we have the world's largest economy.

17
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,220
Many mints worldwide have had to slow their production down.

18
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:28,200
In fact, some have had to shut their production down.

19
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:32,960
We, on the other hand, because of the size of our economy, have had very smooth production capacity.

20
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,400
The U.S. Mint runs production facilities in Denver, San Francisco, and West Point.

21
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:45,300
But for more than two centuries, the heart of its operation has been in downtown Philadelphia.

22
00:01:45,301 --> 00:01:52,300
You can count on this money-making factory to pump out a staggering 11 billion coins each year.

23
00:01:52,301 --> 00:01:56,300
On an average day, we make over 30 million coins.

24
00:01:56,301 --> 00:02:01,300
About half of them are pennies, and the rest are split between nickels, dimes, and quarters.

25
00:02:01,301 --> 00:02:08,300
No parts of the Mint's automated production line are more critical than the dyes from which the coins are stamped.

26
00:02:08,301 --> 00:02:13,300
Creating them is a meticulous and time-consuming challenge.

27
00:02:13,301 --> 00:02:18,300
The process begins with drawings crafted by the Mint staff of sculptor engravers.

28
00:02:18,301 --> 00:02:27,300
Today, we do it all on computers and every image is drawn separately, scanned into the computer.

29
00:02:27,301 --> 00:02:30,300
The lettering and all the text is generated separately.

30
00:02:30,301 --> 00:02:39,300
And we can move it around, increase the size, reduce the size, change the size of the image if the image is too large.

31
00:02:39,301 --> 00:02:41,300
too large.

32
00:02:41,301 --> 00:02:44,300
You know, just kind of tweak it to the point where we never could tweak it before.

33
00:02:44,301 --> 00:02:51,300
The approved drawing is the basis for an oversized model, sculpted first in clay, then

34
00:02:51,301 --> 00:02:52,300
plaster.

35
00:02:52,301 --> 00:02:58,300
Sculpting an enlargement enables the artists to include a greater degree of fine detail.

36
00:02:58,301 --> 00:03:04,300
This is the reverse of the 2006 Nevada State Quarter.

37
00:03:04,301 --> 00:03:05,001
quarter.

38
00:03:05,002 --> 00:03:11,680
I'm putting in the final touches now and I'm using a tool now to put some straight edges on

39
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:15,014
some of these rays of the sun. They need the little cleanup after the casting.

40
00:03:15,015 --> 00:03:16,040
Sometimes there's little

41
00:03:16,041 --> 00:03:18,086
pieces that mar the edge.

42
00:03:18,087 --> 00:03:23,160
We have a very thin parameter of relief that we can work on in

43
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:24,312
something like this.

44
00:03:24,313 --> 00:03:28,400
This is an eight inch blank and I think our maximum relief is around 80

45
00:03:28,401 --> 00:03:30,480
thousandths of an inch and that's the highest point.

46
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:36,820
A crucial step ahead requires a significantly harder replica of the plaster model.

47
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:42,260
So workers craft a rubber mold that they can then use to make an epoxy copy.

48
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:48,060
With the brush we're trying to be sure we don't trap any air bubbles in the detail on the rubber mold

49
00:03:48,061 --> 00:03:51,580
and we'll leave this sit overnight just at air temperature.

50
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:55,080
It'll be hard to the touch in about six hours,

51
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:58,400
and it'll take about 18 hours before it's fully cured,

52
00:03:58,420 --> 00:03:59,780
and we can separate it tomorrow morning.

53
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:06,480
The resulting epoxy model is hard enough to withstand the rigors of this machine.

54
00:04:08,140 --> 00:04:12,300
The Janvier reducing lathes first arrived from France in 1906,

55
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,080
and they're still going strong.

56
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,800
They transfer the contours of the large models with minute precision

57
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:22,680
to the heads of coin-sized steel cylinders.

58
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:30,360
What's going on here is this stylus is actually riding on the surface of the hard epoxy model.

59
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:34,360
And as it moves in and out, this arm also moves in and out,

60
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:39,440
causing this cutter to move in and out and actually trace the image into the steel.

61
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:44,980
This machine is completely mechanical in that there's no computer assistance at all.

62
00:04:44,981 --> 00:04:50,860
It's all done through electrical relays, leather belts, and screws and cone drives.

63
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:57,220
Transferring the features of the model to what's called the reduction hub

64
00:04:57,221 --> 00:05:01,300
takes as long as 12 hours and requires several repetitions.

65
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:06,300
When the hub is ready, it's used to craft the master die.

66
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,820
The master die will be a reverse image of the hub

67
00:05:10,821 --> 00:05:13,700
with recessed rather than raised features.

68
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:17,920
This crucial step allows no margin for error.

69
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:28,060
Any little water will imprint on this, fingerprints will imprint, lint marks, dust, dirt, all will mess this thing up from the get-go.

70
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:39,880
In the die press, the reduction hub is positioned on top of a steel cylinder with a cone-shaped head.

71
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:43,700
The two are precisely aligned with a centering ring.

72
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:50,760
A press descends, flattening the cylinder against the hub.

73
00:05:50,761 --> 00:05:55,040
The cylinder's coned end prevents the cylinder from absorbing all the tonnage at one instant

74
00:05:55,041 --> 00:06:02,380
and allows its metal to flow in and around the hub's relief.

75
00:06:02,381 --> 00:06:06,880
There's still some touch-up work to do, and duplicates for use on the production line

76
00:06:06,881 --> 00:06:08,960
will have to be made.

77
00:06:08,961 --> 00:06:12,220
But what emerges is the stuff that coins are made of.

78
00:06:12,221 --> 00:06:15,520
And the end result is a brand new master die.

79
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:21,400
The process of creating a master die may be slow and methodical,

80
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:26,060
but the coin production it empowers blurs by at a lightning pace.

81
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:33,820
It begins with prefabricated metal strips coiled into rolls 1,500 feet long.

82
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:40,340
The coils are fed into a machine that punches out round discs called blanks.

83
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:46,220
The blanks for pennies come ready-made from the mint supplier.

84
00:06:46,221 --> 00:06:51,220
After blanking, all the blanks go through a furnace where they're heat treated to soften the metal.

85
00:06:51,221 --> 00:06:58,220
We have hard metal to get good sharp blanks, but then we need to soften the metal to be able to strike our coins.

86
00:06:58,221 --> 00:07:03,220
Next, machines wash the blanks to improve the coin's final appearance.

87
00:07:03,221 --> 00:07:10,220
And after other machines dry them, they flow into a device called an upsetting mill.

88
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:17,120
The machine raises a rim around the blank's edge that will be higher than the coin's relief, protecting the design.

89
00:07:18,820 --> 00:07:25,220
A horizontal wheel with a grooved edge rotates near a curved section of steel with a corresponding groove.

90
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:30,180
The blank spins into the channel, which grows progressively narrower.

91
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:36,360
When the channel becomes narrower than the blank itself, the blank flattens at its edge.

92
00:07:36,361 --> 00:07:47,415
That raised edge or rimming helps pre-form the blank so that when it goes into the press and it's actually struck into a coin, we get better fill, better appearance of

93
00:07:47,416 --> 00:07:48,540
the overall coin.

94
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:57,000
Finally, conveyors whisk the blanks to the coining presses and their close encounter with the master working dies.

95
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:01,240
Each press can stamp out 750 coins per minute.

96
00:08:01,241 --> 00:08:07,900
Our coins are struck using two dies that are installed in the stamping presses.

97
00:08:07,901 --> 00:08:11,800
One makes the heads or the obverse, one makes the tails or the reverse.

98
00:08:11,801 --> 00:08:16,220
One is stationary and that's called the anvil, one moves and that's called the hammer.

99
00:08:16,221 --> 00:08:21,000
The blank comes in between, the one die moves and strikes the blank in between that die and

100
00:08:21,001 --> 00:08:26,640
the other die, creating the impression of the coin on both sides at the same time.

101
00:08:26,641 --> 00:08:31,220
To ensure that nothing's gone wrong anywhere on the production line, inspectors scrutinize

102
00:08:31,221 --> 00:08:32,460
the newly stamped coins.

103
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:37,080
It would be an impossible task to inspect each and every coin.

104
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:41,360
What our operators do is they sample ones that are collected in a trap or a reservoir

105
00:08:41,361 --> 00:08:43,100
just outside the press.

106
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:47,900
When they're satisfied that it meets our quality standards, they release the trap,

107
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:52,100
and those coins that are made in a similar time period then flow on through the process

108
00:08:52,101 --> 00:08:53,680
to be counted and bagged.

109
00:08:53,681 --> 00:09:05,240
Each of the bags into which the counted coins fall weighs roughly 2,000 pounds, ready for

110
00:09:05,241 --> 00:09:09,360
shipment to the Federal Reserve.

111
00:09:09,361 --> 00:09:13,420
This efficient production process would have no doubt amazed the founding father, largely

112
00:09:13,421 --> 00:09:16,600
responsible for the Mint's founding.

113
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:22,340
When Thomas Jefferson became America's first Secretary of State in 1789, the new nation's

114
00:09:22,341 --> 00:09:26,840
monetary system floundered in chaos.

115
00:09:26,841 --> 00:09:30,440
No unified form of currency existed.

116
00:09:30,441 --> 00:09:34,580
The individual colonies circulated a wide variety of bills and coins.

117
00:09:34,581 --> 00:09:39,120
And that was only part of the problem.

118
00:09:39,121 --> 00:09:44,620
As the country expanded and we had foreign colonials coming here from France and Britain

119
00:09:44,621 --> 00:09:49,100
and Spain and Portugal and Germany, their currency was oftentimes used as a form of

120
00:09:49,101 --> 00:09:50,100
commerce.

121
00:09:50,101 --> 00:09:53,680
And it was a mess because every colony had its own exchange rate.

122
00:09:54,700 --> 00:10:01,920
So Jefferson really came to understand how over the long term the country just could not conduct its business

123
00:10:01,921 --> 00:10:04,760
and it could not conduct its economy with this kind of a system.

124
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:12,500
He advocated the creation of a decimal coinage system in the United States and the creation of a mint.

125
00:10:12,501 --> 00:10:21,220
America's first Secretary of the Treasury, Alexander Hamilton, shared Jefferson's passion to reform the nation's currency.

126
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:28,740
But the two political rivals, constantly at odds, disagreed about what form that currency should take.

127
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,740
Hamilton believed in banknotes. Jefferson believed in coinage.

128
00:10:33,741 --> 00:10:40,900
So it's not surprising in 1792, when the Mint Act was signed, that coins were placed under the Secretary of State,

129
00:10:40,901 --> 00:10:42,540
where Thomas Jefferson was secretary.

130
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:46,860
The first mint was established and produced coins and not notes,

131
00:10:47,340 --> 00:10:49,660
and that is important in terms of the history of the country.

132
00:10:50,120 --> 00:10:52,580
Notes didn't really start to enter circulation until much later.

133
00:10:54,300 --> 00:10:58,440
The U.S. mint was erected on the site of an abandoned brewery in Philadelphia,

134
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:00,240
then the nation's capital.

135
00:11:01,880 --> 00:11:04,140
Production commenced in 1793.

136
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:08,660
President Washington lived only a few blocks from the first mint,

137
00:11:08,661 --> 00:11:11,920
and it is thought that perhaps Martha Washington donated some of her silver

138
00:11:11,921 --> 00:11:14,340
to the Philadelphia Mintz for some of the first production.

139
00:11:14,780 --> 00:11:16,960
While we don't have any documentation on that,

140
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:19,800
we do know that some of the first copper that was used

141
00:11:19,801 --> 00:11:22,200
was from Paul Revere's manufacturing company

142
00:11:22,201 --> 00:11:24,180
and it was used in some of the first coins.

143
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:27,820
In the old days, there were no clay models.

144
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:31,520
The engravers would actually work directly in the steel.

145
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:33,480
This is what they generally looked like.

146
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,360
And what they would do is they would transfer the drawing to this round image.

147
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:38,480
They'd use an ink.

148
00:11:38,660 --> 00:11:44,640
And then they begin cutting and carving, cutting and carving, cutting and carving, shaping and forming, shaping.

149
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:48,820
And this whole process, I mean, you can see, just look at the detail on this.

150
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:50,320
It's just absolutely amazing.

151
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:51,680
It could take up to six months.

152
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:55,340
In our early days, all the coins were made by hand.

153
00:11:55,580 --> 00:12:01,100
And they would take the metal and they would form it into strips using a rolling machine to get the desired thickness or gauge.

154
00:12:01,101 --> 00:12:08,940
Once they had the strip, each blank was hand-punched from that strip to create a blank.

155
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:14,620
And the blank was then taken to a screw press, placed on top of one die.

156
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:24,320
Another die would be positioned above it, and the screw press would be cranked down in order to stamp or impress the image into that metal.

157
00:12:24,860 --> 00:12:29,260
And in that method, it took them three years to make the first million coins for our nation.

158
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:35,960
The mint's slow pace irritated Congress as it failed to meet production demands.

159
00:12:37,980 --> 00:12:47,300
More tumultuous times lay ahead, especially when a chance discovery in a remote riverbed triggered events destined to rewrite the mint's history.

160
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:56,354
The first United States coin bearing the likeness of an African American was the Booker T.

161
00:12:56,355 --> 00:12:58,180
Washington Memorial Half Dollar.

162
00:12:58,181 --> 00:13:02,660
It was minted from 1946 to 1951.

163
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:06,520
Money will return on Modern Marvels.

164
00:13:09,700 --> 00:13:13,060
We now return to money on Modern Marvels.

165
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:21,780
In 1999, the United States Mint gave the quarter a new look.

166
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:24,860
George Washington still adorns the face.

167
00:13:24,861 --> 00:13:30,780
but you can never be sure which of the United States might be emblazoned on the reverse

168
00:13:30,781 --> 00:13:38,800
every 10 weeks until 2008 the mint will roll out a new quarter honoring a different state

169
00:13:38,801 --> 00:13:45,980
in the order that it joined the union 140 million Americans are caught up in collecting the coins

170
00:13:45,981 --> 00:13:51,700
and the mint continues hammering them out by the ton to keep pace with demand

171
00:13:51,701 --> 00:13:58,700
In the early 19th century, the people's demand for federal coinage was no hobby.

172
00:13:58,701 --> 00:14:05,700
The economic health of their growing nation hinged on the Mint's ability to power a practical system of exchange.

173
00:14:05,701 --> 00:14:09,700
But the Mint's production still lagged.

174
00:14:09,701 --> 00:14:15,700
In the 1820s, the nation's first gold rush complicated its coinage predicament.

175
00:14:15,701 --> 00:14:20,700
After prospectors struck it rich in the mountains of Georgia and the Carolinas,

176
00:14:20,701 --> 00:14:25,700
They had a wealth of gold dust, but no efficient way to convert it to coins.

177
00:14:25,701 --> 00:14:33,700
You could ship it all the way back to Philadelphia where it was melted into coins and sent back,

178
00:14:33,701 --> 00:14:35,700
but this process took some six months.

179
00:14:35,701 --> 00:14:42,700
So you were forced oftentimes as a miner or merchant or whatever to use the gold dust.

180
00:14:42,701 --> 00:14:48,700
A Georgia gunsmith named Templeton Reed had a better idea.

181
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:54,700
He built his own mint, then bought the miner's gold dust and made his own coins.

182
00:14:55,980 --> 00:15:03,180
Under the United States Constitution, only the federal government had the authority to make money.

183
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:08,960
But he was the first of some 40 different individual and private coiners who made coins

184
00:15:08,961 --> 00:15:17,060
to feel the need for a medium of exchange in these gold rush areas when the government failed to do so.

185
00:15:17,061 --> 00:15:26,060
In 1838, Congress finally took action, opening mint branches in North Carolina, Georgia, and Louisiana.

186
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:32,060
It made a lot of sense for the United States government to actually establish mints in those locations.

187
00:15:32,061 --> 00:15:36,060
That would be, I think, in some ways the equivalent of shortening the supply chain,

188
00:15:36,061 --> 00:15:39,060
is the terminology that we use today, as going to the source of your metal.

189
00:15:40,060 --> 00:15:42,060
The precedent was set.

190
00:15:42,061 --> 00:15:48,840
In 1848, when the cry of gold echoed from California, a similar scenario played itself out.

191
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:55,860
Entrepreneurs in San Francisco opened private mints to convert the miners' treasure into coins.

192
00:15:57,880 --> 00:16:04,120
Five years later, the government moved in, absorbed the facilities, and established a branch of the U.S.

193
00:16:04,121 --> 00:16:05,920
mint still in operation today.

194
00:16:05,921 --> 00:16:12,920
In the late 1850s, subsequent gold and silver strikes spawned more branches.

195
00:16:12,921 --> 00:16:17,920
Nevada's Comstock load triggered the creation of the Carson City Mint.

196
00:16:17,921 --> 00:16:22,920
And the Pikes Peak Gold Rush in Colorado gave rise to the mint in Denver.

197
00:16:22,921 --> 00:16:27,920
In the meantime, the mint in Philadelphia had moved to a bigger building.

198
00:16:27,921 --> 00:16:31,920
And its equipment now hissed with the power of steam.

199
00:16:31,921 --> 00:16:38,969
of steam. By 1860, it was pumping out 28 million coins every year.

200
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:41,960
As the government-controlled

201
00:16:41,961 --> 00:16:46,460
coinage was gaining strength, the nation's paper currency, controlled by the private

202
00:16:46,461 --> 00:16:49,813
sector, was flaunting its weaknesses.

203
00:16:49,814 --> 00:16:55,160
Confusion reigned as 1,600 different banks were circulating

204
00:16:55,161 --> 00:16:57,320
7,000 varieties of bills.

205
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:01,240
The further you got away from those issuing banks,

206
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:04,020
they traded at a discount to face value

207
00:17:04,021 --> 00:17:06,160
because of the inconvenience of having to go

208
00:17:06,161 --> 00:17:07,860
take them back to that bank.

209
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:11,380
And then once you got a certain distance away,

210
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:12,740
they weren't worth anything.

211
00:17:13,340 --> 00:17:15,180
No one would accept them because they didn't know

212
00:17:15,181 --> 00:17:17,500
what the creditworthiness of that bank was.

213
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:20,620
You never really knew what the real value

214
00:17:20,621 --> 00:17:23,080
of a Tennessee note would be in Virginia.

215
00:17:23,081 --> 00:17:28,640
And in fact, you would never know if a Richmond note would be even acceptable in Baltimore.

216
00:17:28,940 --> 00:17:37,080
So those kinds of problems really meant that we needed to have a centralized point of issuing currency.

217
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:42,080
A catastrophic conflict finally compelled change.

218
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:49,300
In the 1860s, Congress had realized that they needed some way to finance the Civil War.

219
00:17:49,301 --> 00:17:56,120
and one of the things they did was to authorize the Secretary of the Treasury to print a paper currency,

220
00:17:56,360 --> 00:18:03,100
which was a demand non-interest bearing note, and in fact it was called a demand note.

221
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,220
The Treasury began production in 1861.

222
00:18:08,660 --> 00:18:11,500
Workers operated machinery primitive by today's standards,

223
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:17,460
but the basics of their technique were so sound that they're still being emulated.

224
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:22,020
First, printers applied ink to a metal die.

225
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:28,120
The ink filled the tiny recessed lines and dots of the engraved image, which was carved in reverse.

226
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:33,100
Subsequent steps removed the excess ink from the die's surface.

227
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:37,180
I'm going to take a piece of what they call crinoline, or crinoline.

228
00:18:38,020 --> 00:18:41,120
And what you want to do here is just barely go over the surface,

229
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:45,860
and it's going to take the heaviest part of the excess of that ink away.

230
00:18:45,861 --> 00:18:52,860
After covering his hand in talcum powder, the printer then buffed away whatever traces of ink remained.

231
00:18:52,861 --> 00:19:01,860
As you can see, I'm not actually taking the ink out of the engraving, it's just polishing the surface.

232
00:19:01,861 --> 00:19:08,179
So, if you notice, all of the ink that's left is in the engraving.

233
00:19:08,180 --> 00:19:10,860
The rest of it's on my hand.

234
00:19:10,861 --> 00:19:17,860
The inked dye was then ready for the press that would print the banknote.

235
00:19:17,861 --> 00:19:24,860
The Treasury's earliest models were known as spider presses because of their long radial handles.

236
00:19:24,861 --> 00:19:29,860
They employed a method of printing called intaglio.

237
00:19:29,861 --> 00:19:33,860
And intaglio basically means beneath the surface.

238
00:19:33,861 --> 00:19:39,860
It is where you press the image into the substrate, in this case the paper.

239
00:19:39,861 --> 00:19:45,860
here.

240
00:19:45,861 --> 00:19:48,200
An intaglio press draws the blank paper,

241
00:19:48,201 --> 00:19:52,880
positioned atop the engraving, beneath its drum.

242
00:19:52,881 --> 00:19:56,000
This forces the paper into the die's finely recessed lines

243
00:19:56,001 --> 00:19:58,500
to absorb the ink.

244
00:19:58,501 --> 00:20:03,140
The resulting banknote features a subtle three-dimensional effect.

245
00:20:03,141 --> 00:20:06,680
The surface of the note is slightly raised.

246
00:20:06,681 --> 00:20:10,440
If you pass your hand on any object that's intaglia printed,

247
00:20:10,441 --> 00:20:13,940
you will be able to feel differences and ridges.

248
00:20:14,620 --> 00:20:19,120
And that adds to the distinctiveness of currency.

249
00:20:20,500 --> 00:20:23,060
That trademark feel of American currency

250
00:20:23,061 --> 00:20:26,100
is also a product of the specialized paper on which it's printed.

251
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:31,380
And surprisingly, the main ingredient of that paper

252
00:20:31,381 --> 00:20:33,920
is something you might be wearing right now.

253
00:20:36,680 --> 00:20:43,680
According to the U.S. Mint, the life expectancy of a circulating coin is 30 years.

254
00:20:44,840 --> 00:20:50,840
The Federal Reserve estimates that the average lifespan of a $1 bill is just 22 months.

255
00:20:52,520 --> 00:20:55,120
Money will return on Modern Marvels.

256
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:00,940
We now return to money on Modern Marvels.

257
00:21:00,941 --> 00:21:11,940
We all know money doesn't grow on trees, but it does sprout from, of all things, blue jeans.

258
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:23,140
The discarded scraps from the denim garment manufacturing process are sold by the ton

259
00:21:23,141 --> 00:21:27,060
to make the cotton-based paper on which United States currency is printed.

260
00:21:27,061 --> 00:21:33,060
This is the facility hungrily buying and stockpiling it.

261
00:21:33,061 --> 00:21:37,060
Crane & Company in Dalton, Massachusetts.

262
00:21:37,061 --> 00:21:46,060
Since 1879, this family-owned firm has produced all the specialized paper used to make American banknotes.

263
00:21:46,061 --> 00:21:52,060
There's no getting around the fact that the paper in a banknote is much of the mass.

264
00:21:52,061 --> 00:21:56,060
It's at least 90% of all the material there.

265
00:21:56,061 --> 00:21:59,400
So, it's like the crust in a pizza.

266
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:09,680
Waste cotton comprises about 75% of the material Crane uses to make its banknote paper.

267
00:22:10,980 --> 00:22:13,900
The other 25% is waste flax.

268
00:22:15,700 --> 00:22:21,760
Paper currency must endure a lot of wear and tear, and paper made from wood pulp couldn't survive the abuse.

269
00:22:21,761 --> 00:22:28,160
The fibers in wood pulp are not as long and not as strong as the fibers in cotton.

270
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:37,460
We have nearly pure, highly crystalline cellulose in cotton, so it's much stronger, more brittle,

271
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:44,166
a rattle in the paper when you move it, a crispness, all those characteristics that make U.S.

272
00:22:44,167 --> 00:22:46,960
currency paper so distinct when you feel it.

273
00:22:46,961 --> 00:22:53,860
Achieving that distinctive feel, which counterfeiters find very tricky to duplicate, is the result

274
00:22:53,861 --> 00:22:57,840
of a time-honored and painstaking process.

275
00:22:57,841 --> 00:23:03,300
The first job is to remove some of the natural waxes and oils that are associated with the

276
00:23:03,301 --> 00:23:07,320
otherwise very pure cotton cellulose fiber.

277
00:23:07,321 --> 00:23:12,980
And this and the oils and waxes are generally removed in a pressure cooker, in what we call

278
00:23:12,981 --> 00:23:16,140
a rotary boiler.

279
00:23:16,141 --> 00:23:22,340
For two hours, 6,000 pounds of cotton in the 15-foot wide boiler cooks in a caustic bath

280
00:23:22,341 --> 00:23:28,940
of sodium hydroxide and other chemicals.

281
00:23:28,941 --> 00:23:40,320
A conveyor then dumps the steaming shreds into a pulper that breaks down the cotton

282
00:23:40,321 --> 00:23:43,600
into smaller fibers.

283
00:23:43,601 --> 00:23:50,600
At the same time, water washes away the caustic chemicals still lingering in the material.

284
00:23:50,601 --> 00:23:54,600
Another conveyor advances the cotton pulp toward additional washing stages.

285
00:23:54,601 --> 00:23:58,600
This is the final washing stage here.

286
00:23:58,601 --> 00:24:02,600
This material will be transferred along this conveyor up into our bleaching stage.

287
00:24:02,601 --> 00:24:07,600
We'll take this and we'll make it a whiter material than what it actually is now.

288
00:24:07,601 --> 00:24:18,673
After the bleaching process, we remove the water from the pulp, not 100% dry, but sufficient so that we can stack it on skids and move it around from one place to

289
00:24:18,674 --> 00:24:19,374
another.

290
00:24:19,375 --> 00:24:21,000
And that's what we stockpile.

291
00:24:22,340 --> 00:24:29,180
Several of the ensuing steps are secret and can't be filmed because they involve adding critical anti-counterfeiting features.

292
00:24:29,181 --> 00:24:36,680
Crane will mix red and blue security fibers into the pulp, which will become randomly

293
00:24:36,681 --> 00:24:38,680
distributed throughout the paper.

294
00:24:38,681 --> 00:24:43,920
It will embed a security thread denoting the bill's value at a precise position within

295
00:24:43,921 --> 00:24:46,920
the note.

296
00:24:46,921 --> 00:24:52,480
Crane will also incorporate a watermark that varies by denomination, a faint image mirroring

297
00:24:52,481 --> 00:24:55,260
the portrait on the bill.

298
00:24:55,261 --> 00:25:01,200
The watermark process begins with a pair of bronze dies, used to impress a 3D image on a wire mesh.

299
00:25:03,080 --> 00:25:08,500
You can see here our embossed three-dimensional structures in the wire.

300
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:12,600
This is Abe Lincoln, the subject of the watermark for the $5 bill.

301
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:20,600
The wire mesh is wrapped into the shape of a cylinder that then rotates partially submerged in a tank.

302
00:25:20,601 --> 00:25:27,600
The tank holds the paper, now liquefied into a slurry of 96% water.

303
00:25:27,601 --> 00:25:35,600
As the wire mesh turns, the pattern of the watermark subtly rearranges the fibers collecting on its surface.

304
00:25:35,601 --> 00:25:40,600
The mesh of the wire is so small that it captures the fibers.

305
00:25:40,601 --> 00:25:47,600
So as the fibers are draining, they accumulate in the low areas, they're sloughed off in the raised areas,

306
00:25:47,601 --> 00:25:54,960
and the resulting sheet of paper has this reduction in opacity where it's thin, increased opacity where it's thick,

307
00:25:55,440 --> 00:26:00,620
held up to the light, it produces that multi-tonal shadow look of a watermark.

308
00:26:03,560 --> 00:26:08,280
Like the watermarks, the security threads play a key role in frustrating counterfeiters.

309
00:26:09,460 --> 00:26:12,640
These three-inch wide rolls are 48 threads wide,

310
00:26:13,280 --> 00:26:17,360
and each roll contains enough thread for more than two and a half million notes.

311
00:26:17,600 --> 00:26:25,220
Slitting the roll requires surgical precision, as each of the 48 threads is separated by

312
00:26:25,221 --> 00:26:31,760
a space only 1 1⁄5 of an inch wide.

313
00:26:31,761 --> 00:26:39,440
So we have to introduce a plastic strip whose thickness is roughly equal to a quarter of

314
00:26:39,441 --> 00:26:41,220
the thickness of the paper.

315
00:26:41,221 --> 00:26:46,720
Get it right into the middle, right inside the paper on a continuing basis, thousands

316
00:26:46,721 --> 00:26:52,480
and thousands and thousands of miles with no defects.

317
00:26:52,481 --> 00:26:54,200
That's one of the secret processes

318
00:26:54,201 --> 00:26:58,280
Crane couldn't allow our modern Marvels crew to capture.

319
00:26:58,281 --> 00:27:02,200
After that step, further refinements and drying,

320
00:27:02,201 --> 00:27:08,000
the nearly finished paper emerges as a roll 16 bills wide.

321
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:10,480
To ready the sheets for printing in a later time,

322
00:27:10,481 --> 00:27:14,780
machines then apply a special coating called sizing.

323
00:27:14,781 --> 00:27:20,180
This is an animal glue and chemicals that seals the surface to this paper,

324
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:24,160
because it'd be real porous without it, and the ink would just spread through the fiber.

325
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:27,260
They put this surface sizing on it so it prints well.

326
00:27:28,740 --> 00:27:33,560
After the coated paper dries, cutting machines complete the paper's long odyssey,

327
00:27:34,280 --> 00:27:37,640
dividing the rolls into sheets bearing the templates of 32 bills.

328
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:44,140
What began as raw cotton is now the world's most durable banknote paper.

329
00:27:44,780 --> 00:27:46,580
and a counterfeiter's worst nightmare.

330
00:27:47,960 --> 00:27:51,100
What makes banknote paper really different

331
00:27:51,101 --> 00:27:53,520
is to look at it in transmitted light.

332
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:57,380
This is where the primary security features are revealed.

333
00:27:57,980 --> 00:28:01,800
We have the watermark of Abe Lincoln in every note,

334
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:04,060
and we have the security thread.

335
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:07,700
And the security thread has the denomination of the note.

336
00:28:07,800 --> 00:28:09,700
In this case, it says USA 5.

337
00:28:10,300 --> 00:28:12,100
So in transmitted light, these are visible.

338
00:28:12,101 --> 00:28:13,920
In reflected light, they're gone.

339
00:28:13,921 --> 00:28:18,640
It's a tough assignment for the counterfeiter to produce a feature that fills that bill.

340
00:28:21,740 --> 00:28:26,460
Echoing Tim Crane's analogy, the pizza crust of the banknote is ready for delivery.

341
00:28:28,340 --> 00:28:32,320
It'll be up to the Bureau of Engraving and Printing to add the all-important toppings.

342
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:41,580
This kind of assembly line efficiency began changing the face of America's money factories early in the 20th century.

343
00:28:43,920 --> 00:28:44,630
At the U.S.

344
00:28:44,631 --> 00:28:49,860
Mint, productivity had skyrocketed with the advent of electricity and automation.

345
00:28:51,620 --> 00:28:54,700
The same was true at the Bureau of Engraving and Printing.

346
00:28:55,760 --> 00:29:00,600
Both agencies were helping to energize the economy of a nation emerging as a global power.

347
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:07,000
Each was now filling production orders from the recently established Federal Reserve.

348
00:29:08,580 --> 00:29:13,360
In 1913, President Woodrow Wilson had signed the act creating it.

349
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:16,560
hoping to stabilize America's money and banking system.

350
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:21,900
It provided an elastic currency that could expand and contract

351
00:29:21,901 --> 00:29:24,240
in response to the economy's changing demands.

352
00:29:25,860 --> 00:29:31,620
The Mint and the BEP are the Treasury Department's currency suppliers to the Federal Reserve.

353
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:38,380
The Fed, as it's called, has 12 districts nationwide that monitor America's money supply.

354
00:29:39,660 --> 00:29:43,180
When the amount of cash in a district falls below acceptable levels,

355
00:29:43,840 --> 00:29:48,180
the Fed places an order with the Mint and the BEP to make and deliver more money.

356
00:29:51,080 --> 00:29:56,740
During the Fed's early years, the money it managed finally began to resemble what we take for granted today.

357
00:29:58,760 --> 00:30:03,800
Until the 20th century, no likeness of a president had ever graced an American coin,

358
00:30:03,801 --> 00:30:06,140
a tradition inspired by George Washington.

359
00:30:07,620 --> 00:30:10,540
Washington had rejected the Mint's plans to use his portrait,

360
00:30:10,541 --> 00:30:14,640
wanting no part of a practice exercised by European monarchs.

361
00:30:16,020 --> 00:30:19,660
And it really wasn't until the introduction of the Abraham Lincoln penny

362
00:30:19,661 --> 00:30:22,040
in which you saw a president on a coin.

363
00:30:22,600 --> 00:30:25,580
Shortly thereafter, you saw other presidents show up on coins as well.

364
00:30:25,720 --> 00:30:29,160
You see Thomas Jefferson, and of course we have Franklin Delano Roosevelt,

365
00:30:29,480 --> 00:30:32,080
John F. Kennedy on our coins today,

366
00:30:32,081 --> 00:30:34,040
and George Washington himself on the quarter.

367
00:30:35,540 --> 00:30:38,780
America's paper currency also got a 20th century facelift.

368
00:30:40,540 --> 00:30:47,340
Until 1929, federal banknotes were a half-inch wider and nearly an inch and a half longer than today's.

369
00:30:49,520 --> 00:30:53,700
Designing a smaller note enabled more notes to be printed for every turn of the press.

370
00:30:55,840 --> 00:30:59,460
The dimensions of our bills haven't changed in three quarters of a century,

371
00:31:00,100 --> 00:31:03,040
but virtually everything else about them has.

372
00:31:03,041 --> 00:31:10,940
Making the new generation of currency combines old-school craftsmanship and cutting-edge technology.

373
00:31:16,700 --> 00:31:23,880
A new American banknote can be folded forward and backward at least 4,000 times before it tears.

374
00:31:25,160 --> 00:31:28,160
Money will return on Modern Marvels.

375
00:31:28,161 --> 00:31:35,160
We now return to money on Modern Marvels.

376
00:31:35,161 --> 00:31:45,160
Some of us might have doubts about how wisely the American government spends money.

377
00:31:45,161 --> 00:31:49,160
But you can be certain it's well-equipped to create it.

378
00:31:49,161 --> 00:31:53,709
The Bureau of Engraving and Printing's facilities in Washington, D.C.

379
00:31:53,710 --> 00:31:55,160
and Fort Worth, Texas,

380
00:31:55,161 --> 00:31:59,160
turn out 35 million banknotes every day,

381
00:31:59,161 --> 00:32:03,160
with a face value of about $635 million.

382
00:32:03,161 --> 00:32:09,160
We have to produce billions and billions and billions of this product.

383
00:32:09,161 --> 00:32:12,160
It has to be easily recognizable.

384
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:16,160
It has to be consistent billions and billions of times.

385
00:32:16,161 --> 00:32:20,160
But it also has to be exceedingly difficult for someone else to produce.

386
00:32:20,161 --> 00:32:23,160
Now that to me is a challenge.

387
00:32:23,161 --> 00:32:31,160
The BEP, like its sister agency, the US Mint, relies on an elite staff of engravers to craft its master dies.

388
00:32:32,160 --> 00:32:35,160
The art of engraving itself hasn't changed much.

389
00:32:35,161 --> 00:32:38,160
The tools are very similar to what they were 400 years ago.

390
00:32:39,160 --> 00:32:42,160
The primary engraving tool is a Buren, her graver.

391
00:32:43,160 --> 00:32:49,160
It's just a straight steel shaft that's polished on two sides with a bevel on the end of it.

392
00:32:49,161 --> 00:32:56,960
bevel on the end of it and you just put it in a small holder and push with the

393
00:32:56,961 --> 00:33:02,180
palm of your hand through the metal and it cuts a tiny little curl out at a time

394
00:33:02,181 --> 00:33:07,700
you can control the depth and width of the line with astonishing accuracy and

395
00:33:07,701 --> 00:33:11,660
you're doing this pretty much in miniature version and you're doing this

396
00:33:11,661 --> 00:33:17,660
backwards in reverse and the fascinating thing is if you make your big mistake

397
00:33:17,661 --> 00:33:23,540
nine months of work will go down the drain in just one cut. Making a mistake

398
00:33:23,541 --> 00:33:30,420
in original die is a tragedy. I would have to fix that by using a scraper. I

399
00:33:30,421 --> 00:33:35,000
would have to hollow it out in that area until I made a smooth little depression,

400
00:33:35,001 --> 00:33:41,760
then take the engraving die, flip it over, hammer the back of it so that it would

401
00:33:41,761 --> 00:33:46,520
raise a bump on the surface. I would then take a series of little honing stones

402
00:33:46,521 --> 00:33:53,520
over the top of the surface and smooth it back and forth until it was absolutely flat.

403
00:33:53,521 --> 00:33:59,520
Then take furnishing tools to re-polish the area, then mark it back in and re-cut it accurately.

404
00:33:59,521 --> 00:34:04,520
You end up destroying a good deal of work around the error.

405
00:34:04,521 --> 00:34:12,520
Once completed successfully, however, a skilled engraver's handiwork is a banknote's first line of defense against counterfeiters.

406
00:34:12,521 --> 00:34:26,521
If I were to ask any one of my banknote engravers to engrave the very same design, exactly the same, twice, that person can't do it because of the subtle human nature

407
00:34:29,324 --> 00:34:30,640
of designing.

408
00:34:30,641 --> 00:34:38,520
The human aspect of engraving a banknote is unique to that individual process and unique to that time.

409
00:34:38,521 --> 00:34:50,920
That exacting, detailed, 19th century craftsmanship is the cornerstone of how we build our counterfeiting deterrent strategy.

410
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:59,860
The uniquely etched master dies are duplicated in plastic to craft the Intaglio Press's 32 sheet plates.

411
00:35:00,780 --> 00:35:03,780
They're welded together in individual rows, eight to a row.

412
00:35:04,620 --> 00:35:05,740
One row is welded.

413
00:35:06,040 --> 00:35:07,060
They're inspected again.

414
00:35:07,061 --> 00:35:10,480
check to make sure it's the right size

415
00:35:10,481 --> 00:35:12,220
and then it goes to the second row

416
00:35:12,221 --> 00:35:15,320
and we do that so we'll end up with four of these rows

417
00:35:15,321 --> 00:35:17,700
and then it comes time to put it together.

418
00:35:17,920 --> 00:35:20,640
Then it's one long weld and we put all four of them together

419
00:35:20,641 --> 00:35:23,880
and you can see on the back, you can see where it's welded.

420
00:35:25,500 --> 00:35:27,480
Each one was welded individually first

421
00:35:27,481 --> 00:35:30,300
and then welded three times across to make four rows.

422
00:35:31,720 --> 00:35:34,980
After a plate maker coats the plastic plates in chromium

423
00:35:35,780 --> 00:35:37,040
The engravers inspect them.

424
00:35:38,320 --> 00:35:40,420
Then the plates are secured to the presses.

425
00:35:41,780 --> 00:35:44,000
But we're not quite ready to fire them up.

426
00:35:46,000 --> 00:35:48,120
Before the intaglio printing begins,

427
00:35:48,121 --> 00:35:52,680
some denominations of the blank 32-subject sheets are offset printed.

428
00:35:54,160 --> 00:35:58,940
The process adds subtle colors, symbols of freedom, and micro-printing,

429
00:35:59,560 --> 00:36:02,440
forming a new layer of complexity to foil counterfeiters.

430
00:36:04,980 --> 00:36:09,280
Let's take a look inside the massive printer that applies the offset hues and images.

431
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:11,620
The Simultan Press.

432
00:36:12,760 --> 00:36:16,140
The ink is applied from the rollers onto the plates.

433
00:36:16,740 --> 00:36:19,160
The image is then transferred to the blanket cylinder.

434
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:25,680
The paper comes between the two blanket cylinders, being the face and back, printing both sides simultaneously.

435
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:30,600
This is the feeder end of the Simultan Press.

436
00:36:30,700 --> 00:36:32,880
This is where we put our offset images on it.

437
00:36:32,881 --> 00:36:39,480
The paper is fed down the elongated feed board, down into the prefront lays, and this is where

438
00:36:39,481 --> 00:36:44,120
it actually goes into the printing units to print both sides at the same time.

439
00:36:44,121 --> 00:36:50,060
We're running the orange ink, the green ink, and the blue ink, and right now I'm about

440
00:36:50,061 --> 00:36:52,820
to put the green ink in the inkwell.

441
00:36:52,821 --> 00:36:57,560
We refill the ink fountain approximately every four hours, which is the equivalent of about

442
00:36:57,561 --> 00:37:02,520
30,000 printed sheets.

443
00:37:02,521 --> 00:37:08,120
During offset printing, vigilant pressmen keep a close eye on quality.

444
00:37:08,121 --> 00:37:12,640
We will extract a sheet during the running of the press and bring them over to the Auto

445
00:37:12,641 --> 00:37:16,640
Smart Scanner and read all the color swatches for ink density.

446
00:37:16,641 --> 00:37:21,620
He's looking at the orange and the green and he also reviews his blue.

447
00:37:21,621 --> 00:37:22,980
We have to monitor that very closely.

448
00:37:22,981 --> 00:37:27,480
Our tolerance and standards for our currency production is very, very tight.

